@mercuryworkshop/scramjet 1.1.0
  https://github.com/MercuryWorkshop/scramjet

  Recorded because the sources disagree, and someone will check again:

    - the pinned version, 1.1.0 (published 2026-04-27), declares
      "license": "MIT" in its npm metadata
    - the tarball it ships contains the 661-line GNU Affero General Public
      License v3.0 text
    - upstream's own package.json today declares "AGPL-3.0-only"
    - GitHub's API reports license: null, because it detects licences from
      LICENSE FILES and the repo has none. That is not an absence of licence,
      and should not be read as one

  The project relicensed part-way through its 2.x alpha series:

      1.0.0 – 1.0.2        ISC              2024-06 → 2024-07
      2.0.0-alpha …        MIT              2025-09 → 2026-05-16
      1.1.0                MIT              2026-04-27   <- pinned here
      2.0.6-alpha onward   AGPL-3.0-only    2026-05-24 →

  Since this project is itself AGPL-3.0, the question is moot in either
  direction: an AGPL dependency is compatible with an AGPL work, and an MIT
  dependency always was. No obligation is created that this project's own
  licence does not already impose.

  This matters only if this project is ever relicensed to something permissive.
  In that case 1.1.0's MIT declaration would have to be confirmed with upstream
  before shipping, and the version must not be advanced past 2.0.5-alpha.

  The version is pinned exactly rather than with a caret. A range would let a
  routine dependency bump cross the 2.0.6-alpha boundary silently.

